WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.120
♪ [music] ♪

00:00:05.833 --> 00:00:07.657
Give me one minute, and I'll show you

00:00:07.657 --> 00:00:10.733
how Virtual Private Cloud
serves as a foundation

00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:12.260
for your network in the cloud.

00:00:13.386 --> 00:00:17.994
Virtual Private Cloud, or VPC,
is the managed networking service

00:00:17.994 --> 00:00:19.872
for your Google Cloud infrastructure.

00:00:19.872 --> 00:00:23.008
You could think of it as similar
to a traditional physical network,

00:00:23.008 --> 00:00:25.403
except it's virtualized 
within Google Cloud.

00:00:25.688 --> 00:00:28.806
VPCs on Google Cloud have a global scope,

00:00:28.806 --> 00:00:32.489
so a single VPC can span
across multiple regions

00:00:32.489 --> 00:00:34.231
all over the world, securely,

00:00:34.231 --> 00:00:36.640
without communicating 
across the public internet.

00:00:37.155 --> 00:00:40.146
VPCs are shareable between
Google Cloud projects

00:00:40.146 --> 00:00:42.453
or even organizations in some cases.

00:00:42.859 --> 00:00:45.716
That means you can use 
a single shared VPC

00:00:45.716 --> 00:00:47.221
for your entire organization

00:00:47.221 --> 00:00:51.643
so that teams isolated within projects
can maintain a shared private IP space

00:00:51.643 --> 00:00:54.019
to access commonly used services.

00:00:54.537 --> 00:00:56.096
Need to expand your network?

00:00:56.096 --> 00:00:57.125
No problem.

00:00:57.125 --> 00:01:00.850
Google Cloud VPC lets you increase
the IP space of any subnet

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.111
without any downtime or workload shutdown,

00:01:04.111 --> 00:01:07.303
giving you the flexibility to grow 
your network to meet your needs.

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:12.021
By default, your Google Cloud projects 
are ready to roll with a default VPC

00:01:12.021 --> 00:01:15.292
configured with multiple subnets 
created across multiple regions.

00:01:15.292 --> 00:01:18.021
You can use these subnets
or you can create your own,

00:01:18.021 --> 00:01:20.152
allowing you to configure 
the number of subnets,

00:01:20.152 --> 00:01:23.309
their corresponding regions, 
and their IP address ranges.

00:01:23.754 --> 00:01:26.179
Do you have existing infrastructure 
outside of Google Cloud

00:01:26.179 --> 00:01:27.948
that you want to connect to your VPC?

00:01:28.341 --> 00:01:31.131
There are multiple ways to create 
a hybrid-networking solution

00:01:31.131 --> 00:01:33.679
using Dedicated or Partner Interconnect,

00:01:33.679 --> 00:01:34.955
Cloud VPN,

00:01:34.955 --> 00:01:38.163
or even via partner-supported
SD-WAN connections.

00:01:39.288 --> 00:01:42.466
Keep things on your network safe 
and secure with Packet Mirroring.

00:01:42.466 --> 00:01:44.230
This allows you to mirror all traffic

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:46.963
between your compute instances in your VPC

00:01:46.963 --> 00:01:48.878
so that you can inspect and analyze it

00:01:48.878 --> 00:01:50.627
for application performance issues

00:01:50.627 --> 00:01:53.204
or to detect threats and anomalies.

00:01:53.468 --> 00:01:55.065
So, next time you're looking into

00:01:55.065 --> 00:01:56.841
how you can build
your network in the cloud,

00:01:56.841 --> 00:01:59.607
take a minute to check out 
Google Cloud VPCs

00:01:59.607 --> 00:02:01.735
or Private Managed Network in the cloud.

00:02:02.212 --> 00:02:06.487
To learn more, check out
<i>cloud.google.com/vpc</i>

